Welsh National National Assembly fear they will be called muppets
Concrepisctured among some members of the National Assembly. This is the result of a proposal to change their title to the Welsh Parliament (MMAP).
The idea arose in connection with plans to reshape the assembly into the Welsh Parliament.
Members of the assembly from all sides of the political scene are worrying that they may become the subject of mockery.
One of the Labor Party MPs expressed concerns about his party, saying the new name rhymes with the Welsh words “twP” and “Pwp”.
For non-Welonia readers: in Welsh, the word “twp” stands for a fool, and the word “pwp” means faeces.
One Politic of Plaid Cymru stated that his group is “unsatisfied” with the proposals and presented other solutions.
Welsh Conservative Party member said his party was “open” to re-named. However, he pointed out that the word “MWP” is very close to the word “muppet”.
In this case, the Welsh letter “w” is pronounced similarly to the letter “u” in the English dialect of Yorkshire.
The committee acting within the assembly, which is currently working on a bill amending the name, took the following stance: "Naturally the final decision on the future title of the members of the assembly will belong to themselves".
The Government of Wales Act of 2017 gave the Welsh National Assembly the right to renable its name.
In June, the committee published the results of a public consultation on the proposal. Renaming the assembly to the Welsh Parliament received broad support.
In addition, the committee endorsed the proposal to change the title of members of the assembly to the “Member of the Welsh Parliament”, i.e. WMP in English, although the abbreviation “MWP” enjoyed the greatest popularity among the public.
Difficulties with reaching a consensual consensus are a nuisance for Elin Jones, president of the assembly, which should present the bill in the coming weeks.
The law introducing reforms will also take into account other changes in the work of the assembly, including the principles of dismissal of members and the structure of the co-bike system.
The members of the National Assembly will be able to vote on their future title when discussing the law.
Macedonians vote in referendum to renaming the country
Next Sunday, the voters will decide whether their country will soon be called the Republic of North Macedonia.
The popular vote is intended to prevent a decades-durgonal dispute with the neighboring Greece, which houses a region called Macedonia.
The government in Athens has long argued that the name of their northern neighbor constitutes a territorial claim and has repeatedly objected to attempts to join the European Union and NATO.
Lavrov, the president of Macedonia and opponent of the referendum on renaming, said he would disregard the results of the vote.
Proponents of the plebiscite, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, maintain that change is necessary to enable the state to join the European Union and NATO.
