Sainsbury’s retail chain Plans to Expandiaration in the British Cosmetics Market
Sainsbury’s shoals glove in stores such as Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams by introducing cosmetic departments into their supermarkets with style referring to department stores, as well as specially-trained retailers.
As part of a significant investment in the British cosmetics market – which is worth 2.8 billion pounds and is constantly developing despite declines in the fashion and household goods industries – in the 11 stores belonging to the chain there will be larger racks with cosmetic products.
The return to the cosmetics industry stems from the search for ways to develop space previously occupied by televisions, microwaves and household goods.
According to Sainsbury’s, the chain will double the number of cosmetic products and offer up to 3,000 articles. For the first time there will be goods such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. Pawpaw.
L’Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees currently sold products will also receive more places and separate areas similar to those known from Boots stores.
In addition, the chain of supermarkets is planning to refresh its offer of make-up products Boutique. From now on, most items will be vegan-fashionable, which is increasingly being demanded by younger customers.
In addition, perfume retailer Fragrance Shop will create test points in two Sainsbury’s supermarkets. The first was opened last week in Croydon, south of London, and the second will be built in Selly Oak, in Birmingham later this year.
The prevalence of online sales and daily purchases of small amounts of food in local stores means that supermarkets need to more encourage customers to visit.
Mike Coupe, CEO of Sainsbury’s, said sales outlets would increasingly resemble commodity homes as the network tries to fight discounters such as Aldi or Lidl, offering more services and non-food products.
Sainsbury’s has placed Argos outlets in hundreds of stores and has been introducing a number of Habitat points since the acquisition of both companies two years ago. According to the company, this contributes to increasing grocery sales and increasing the profitability of investments.
The previous attempt to re-organize sections with cosmetic products and pharmaceuticals resulted in failure.
At the beginning of the 21st century, Sainsbury’s created a joint-venture company from Boots. However, the cooperation was concluded as a result of the conflict over the division of revenues from pharmacies operating in supermarkets.
“Sainsbury’s introduces a new strategy following the sale of 281 pharmacies to Celesio, the owner of the Lloyds Pharmacy network for 125 million pounds three years ago.
According to the reports, Lloyds will contribute to the implementation of the plan by introducing an expanded range of luxury skin care brands, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy, to the chain’s four stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Chief Commercial Officer Sainsbury’s, said: “We’ve re-transparated the look and feel of cosmetic departments in order to improve customer experiences.
“We’ve invested in special training for collaborators so they can help consumers when shopping.
“Our brands are able to meet all their needs, and a friendly environment and convenient locations allow us to attract the attention of customers looking for cosmetic products, as well as to challenge existing ways of shopping.”
