John-Ethreat: Language Skills Necessary for the Citizens of the World
Uninterdependent schools in Scotland are constantly enjoying their scientific achievements – in 2018 they were able to boast further excellent exam results, supported by individual and collective successes in sports, arts, music and other social endeavors.
Represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), attended by more than 30,000 pupils across Scotland, aim to provide the best level of service provided to pupils and parents.
Independent schools are designed to prepare pupils for further and higher education, their chosen career path and to indicate their role as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector with the opportunity to develop and implement a tailor-to-chienage curriculum, we can see that modern languages continue to be popular and desirable electives in schools.
Nelson Mandela once said, “If you say to the other person in the language that he understands, it hits his head.
“If you talk to him in his own language, it goes to his heart.”
This is a clear reminder that we can’t just relive on English when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
The results of the last exam this year show that the languages occupy the first place in league rankings, with the highest reporting rates occurring for independent schools.
A total of 68 per cent of pupils who have learned foreign languages obtained a higher A-rated.
Figures comproiled from 74 SCIS member schools found that 72 per cent of pupils achieved a higher A-in-Hodgage in Mandarin and 72 per cent of German learners, 69 per cent of French learners and 63 per cent of Spanish learners have also received an A-denser.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as essential skills that children and adolescents will undoubtedly need in the future.
Currently, language learning as electives is conducted in the same area as STEM subjects (Scientists, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics) as part of curriculing programs in independent schools and elsewhere.
A 2014 study conducted by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills found that employers’ lack of language profici Novlevies accounted of 17 per cent’s lack of language proficiency.
That’s why language skills are increasingly becoming a necessity to prepare young people to start a professional career.
These skills are necessary in a globalised world with a growing number of jobs that require knowledge of languages.
A lifelong ability to learn a second language will provide a huge advantage in the future—revealing a path of professional development.
Possibility of direct communication with people from other countries automatically increases the odds of multi-linguent over the competition.
According to the YouGov poll from 2013, attended by more than 4,000 young British adults, 75% of them could not speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, and French was the only language whose percentage of people using were double-digit, i.e. 15%.
That’s why investing in teaching foreign languages is so important for today’s children.
Familiar knowledge of several languages, in particular the languages of developing economies, will provide children with a better chance of finding suitable employment.
In Scotland, every school will be different in terms of the languages taught.
A number of schools will focus on more classic modern languages, while others will teach languages considered to be the most important for the United Kingdom in the perspective of 2020, such as Mandarin and Japanese.
Irrespective of the child's interests, there will always be multiple languages for choice in independent schools, which will be instructed staff specialized in this area.
Scientist independent schools are committed to creating a teaching environment that will prepare children and arm them with the skills necessary to succeed – no matter what the future prepares for them.
There is now no denying that in the international business environment, languages continue to be crucial for the future of the country, and so this must be reflected in education.
Modern languages should be regarded as “international communication skills”.
Independent schools will continue to offer Scottish young people a large choice, diversity and level.
It's just the right thing.
John Edward, President of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
