“Last year, the most important thing for me was winning a gold medal,” he said.
“And you know what? There are more important things than Olympic gold medals.
“My Olympic Gold Medal is Robbie.”
His son bears the same name as his father, Robert, who in 2011 died at the age of the age of 40 when he was diagnosed with cerebral cancer.
Daley said that his father didn’t accept that he’d been dying, and one of the last things that he asked was if they already had tickets to the London Olympics in 2012 — he wanted to take a first row.
“I couldn’t have told him, ‘You wouldn’t be with us to be in the front row, Dad,” he said.
“Speaking his hand when he hadn’t stopped breathing. It wasn’t when he stopped breathing and he died, I realized he wasn’t invincible,” he said.
In the following year, Daley took part in the 2012 Olympics and won bronze.
“I knew this was what I’ve dreamed of all my life — jumping into the water in front of a crowd of people in the Olympics — it’s the best feeling possible,” he said.
It also inspired him to choose his first song—proud by Heather Small—which accompanied him in preparations for the Olympic Games and which continues to give him a goose bump.
