The New German War Ship Retold
The newest frigate of the German navy should be put into use in 2014 to replace aging Cold War naval ships, but this won’t take place at least until next year due to flawed streaky deals and rising at an intimidating rate – according to local media.
"Rheinland-Pfalz", the main ship of the new Baden-Wuerttemberg frigate, has been postponed to the first half of 2019, Die Zeit, citing a military spokeswoman.
The ship should have been incorporated into the navy in 2014, but as a result of the problems that arose after delivery, it was necessary to make changes to this ambitious project.
The Four Baden-Wuerttemberg-Scienttemberg-Uerttemberg-class units commissioned in 2007 will be replaced by Bremen’s arrogant frigates.
Of course, they will be equipped with a powerful gun, a number of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as technological solutions that make it impossible to detect, among other things, provide a reduction in radar, infrared and acoustic traces.
Other important features include longer intervals between maintenance – the exploitation of new frigates should be possible for up to two years away from their home ports.
Continuous retardations, however, mean that these modern naval navies, which are supposed to enable Germany to respond to situations abroad at the start of the service will be obsolete – Die Zeit notes.
Unremnable frigate F125 made headlines last year when the German navy formally declined to put the ship into operation and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It was the first time the navy returned the ship to the shipyard after delivery.
Not much has been known about the reasons behind the return, but the German media reported a number of key “watches of software and hardware” that made the ship useless in the combat mission.
Particularly important were the flaws of the software, as Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships are to be handled by a crew of about 120 seafarers, which is half as much as for older Bremen-class frigates.
It also turned out that the ship is much too heavy, which reduces its performance and limits the ability of the navy to carry out future modernizations by the navy.
It is believed that the 7,000-hundred-hundred-throat “Rheinland-Pfalz” is thought to be twice heavier than ships of a similar class used by the Germans during World War II.
In addition to defective equipment, the cost of the entire project also becomes a problem – including crew training.
It is said to have reached a staggering $3.1 billion ($3.6 billion), while the initial cost was EUR 2,2 billion.
Dislegation of the new frigates are of particular importance in the light of recent warnings that the strength of the German naval fleet is shrinking.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the German parliament's defense committee, admitted that the navy "will eventually be defeatable ships".
He also said that this problem increased over time as a result of discontinuation of old vessels without the provision of replacement ships.
In addition, expressed his dissatisfashion with the fact that none of Baden-Wuerttemberg class frigates could be incorporated into the naval fleet.
The National Trust Eavesdruping The Secret Life of Bats
A new study carried out at a property in the Scottish Highlands is designed to show how bats use the landscape to obtain food.
The findings are expected to shed new light on the preservation of these exceptional flying mammary mammmals and will help steer future conservation efforts.
Research conducted by scientists from the National Trust for Scotland will cover common and small dwarves, as well as Bronze halves with long-whiners and bats from a red-swatt-sufficient in Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be deployed in key locations around the accelerators for full-season tracking of bat activity.
NHS workers and volunteers will also carry out field research using portable detectors.
Experimental sound analysis of all recordings is aimed at determining the frequency of signals sent by batters and how individual genres are behaved.
Subsequently, a habitat map and a report will be compiled to create a detailed picture of their behavior on the scale of the landscape.
Rob Dewar, a conservation adviser at NTS, hopes the results will reveal which habitat areas are most important for bats and how they are used by each species.
This information will help to identify the benefits associated with habitat management, e.g. meadows formation, as well as the best ways to maintain forest areas for bats and other related species.
Populated bat populations in Scotland and across the United Kingdom have declined significantly over the last century.
They are endangered by construction and development works that have an impact on tendencies and habitat loss.
Wind turbines and luminous installations can also pose a threat, just like flops for flies and some chemicals used in building materials and even domestic cats.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, due to nocturnal maggot customs, their ears are more helpful than the eyes in catching precipitacies.
They use a complex echlocation technique to find worms and obstacles on their way.
The NTS organization, which is in charge of more than 270 anti-fidential buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes the bats very seriously.
It consists of ten trained experts, who regularly carry out tests, gense inspections and sometimes even rescue operations.
“The organization has even set up the first and the only-equivocycated Bat Reservoir Reserve in Dumfries in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight out of Scotland’s 10-year-old species.
According to David Thompson, the property's manager, says she's an ideal territory for bats.
“Threave owns a splendid area for bats,” he said.
“We’ve got old buildings, a lot of old trees and excellent habitats.
However, there are still a lot of unknowns when it comes to bats. The work we are doing here and in other possessions will help us to better understand what they need in order to thrive.”
It emphasizes the importance of control for the presence of bats before proceeding to carry out maintenance works in the estates, because the inadvertent destruction of a single roster can kill up to 400 females and young, which can lead to the extinction of the entire local population.
Do bats are protected — killing, harassing or intercriminating, and it’s illegal to destroy them.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a scamindan officer at the Bat Conservation Trust, has encouraged the public to provide assistance.
She said: “We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and for many species, we don’t even know how their populations do.”
